  "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=6$ is equal to its position at $t=3$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(6)=x(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(6) & =2+{\\int }_{3}^{6}(6t-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{[3{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =2+[3(6)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}]-[3(3)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(3)^{3}] \\\\  & =2+36-18 \\\\  & =2+18 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the position of the particle at $t=6$ is $x=20.$",
